    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on the year end stuff and i'm a little stuck on a depreciation calc. can you walk me through how to do straight-line depreciation for a piece of equipment? it cost 45000, has a salvage value of 5000, and a useful life of 8 years. just need the step by step so i can make sure i'm doing it right for the reports.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a step-by-step guide to calculating straight-line depreciation:\n\n1. Calculate the total depreciable amount by subtracting the salvage value from the cost: $45,000 - $5,000 = $40,000\n2. Divide the total depreciable amount by the number of years in the equipment's useful life (8 years): $40,000 / 8 = $5,000 per year\n3. That's your annual depreciation expense, which you can record as a direct cost on your income statement or on a depreciation schedule for tax purposes.\n\nI hope this helps, and please let me know if you have any questions or need further clarification!",
